What changed. Nothing in the repository; no commit, push or pull request. One decision card (id fm-motion-plan) carries the answer, four figures, a six-column spec table, an interactive preview with captured settings, six numbered questions with my answers pre-picked, assumptions, eight checkable criteria, exclusions and three shippable pieces in merge order. Its actions: "Choose reveal only" (default), "Choose reveal and continuity", and a keeps-open "Use these settings" that carries the preview's snapshot and the question picks.

The proposed spec, as it stands before the captain's replies (direction A, reveal only). Rules adopted from transitions.dev's own words: closes are faster and quieter than opens and never delayed; overshoot belongs to entrances only; match on usage, never on the nearest number; every motion ships a reduced-motion guard; nothing depends on motion alone; compositor-friendly properties only. Its scale (80 to 500 ms), recipes, blur, scale and bouncy easings are not imported; the design skill's tokens stay the one vocabulary: --dur-fast 160 ms for in-place reveals, --dur-med 360 ms for a surface's entrance, --dur-slow 700 ms for dissolves, --ease-out for entrances, --ease-drift for state and bars. Triggers and motion: the shown card changes (arrival to the front, paging, a close revealing the next) fades in and rises 4 px over --dur-med, keyed by card id so a newer card restarts it, never the card's height or the pager badge; the task pane opens with opacity 0 to 1 over --dur-fast, tie and notch with it, never its top (an e2e pins that); a disclosure body (Disclosure, Projects accordion, Tasks group, record and card-body details) fades in and settles 4 px down from its header over --dur-fast, close instant and unmounting as today, height never tweened; a settings section fades in over --dur-fast and the tab label's color eases, no sliding underline; a feed line born after its document fades in opacity-only over --dur-fast, the boot snapshot and a window slide's older rows never; a reply or the working row fades in and rises 4 px over --dur-med; a frame's first draw (panel body, card body) fades from 0 to 1 over --dur-fast on onDrawn, never on a re-push or at first paint. Everything that eases today (hovers, carets, dock widths, stage lift, the Firstmate's dim and blur, the whisper, quota bars, the switch, the picture viewer) is unchanged. Closes and dismissals are instant everywhere; state commits before motion (aria, disabled, DOM on the click) and nothing waits on transitionend; entrances are keyframes on keyed nodes so an interruption restarts, never queues. Reduced motion: one rule in tokens/effects.css zeroes --dur-fast, --dur-med and --dur-slow under prefers-reduced-motion in both the shell and every frame (the frame stylesheet already imports that file), replacing the five per-site rules; a new --dur-fade token (160 ms) is left alone so alpha-only fades that carry continuity (the whisper) can opt in. Boundaries: opacity and transform only, no filter or new backdrop-filter, no height, width or grid-row tweens, no will-change, no perpetual motion, no motion at launch, feed:render and now:render budgets unchanged. Questions with my proposed answers: 1 fades survive under reduced motion; 2 surfaces 360 ms, in-place 160 ms; 3 no directional paging; 4 static first paint; 5 revise the skill's motion line to name each token's use; 6 frames included through the shared stylesheet only. Held for the next round: the whisper's dissolve length under reduced motion if fades survive; the tab underline if B is chosen. Assumed: the design skill outranks transitions.dev; the character, crew orbit and Crystal are untouched; card bodies keep rendering verbatim; Chromium 150 needs nothing newer; tests that read state at the first frame after a click keep passing. Pieces in merge order: (1) skill rules, --dur-fade and the one reduced-motion rule with lint and unit tests; (2) reveals on the stage (card, reply, task pane); (3) reveals in rails, settings and frames (Disclosure, Projects accordion, sections, Tasks group, fresh Activity rows, frame first draw, details in frame bodies); (4) only if B: underline slide, directional paging, size tweens.

Verified. transitions.dev read from its published skill source and accessibility section; the app audited by grep over src/renderer (about thirty motion sites, all on tokens; five separate reduced-motion rules), the design skill's tokens and readme, docs on the translucent window and composer, the whisper and task-pane prototypes and their commits, and the e2e tests that pin motion behaviour. The card body was rendered with the design stylesheet in headless Chromium: tag balance, minimum 11 px SVG text, the script and the preview's snapshot (including the reduced-motion toggle) all checked. No open task overlaps this one.

Risk. Low; nothing ships from this task.

