    <text x="0" y="195">--dur-slow 700</text><rect x="120" y="186" width="350" height="9" rx="2" fill="var(--accent)"/>
  </g>
  <line x1="120" y1="206" x2="470" y2="206" stroke="var(--line-2)"/>
  <g font-family="var(--font-mono)" font-size="11" fill="var(--fg-3)">
    <text x="120" y="213">0</text><text x="245" y="213">250</text><text x="370" y="213">500</text><text x="455" y="213">700 ms</text>
  </g>
</svg>
<figcaption>Bars are to one time scale. The app's tokens are the lit row: they stay; the shorter scale is not imported.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>What is inference on my side: that its recipes are tuned for busy product and marketing surfaces (blur, scale, bounces, shimmers, toasts) and fit a calm stage badly; the design skill already forbids bounces, spinning chrome and new blur layers, and names 360 to 700&thinsp;ms as the house pace. What the two agree on, and what this plan adopts, is the rule set: asymmetry, usage over number, compositor-only properties, a reduced-motion guard on everything, nothing conveyed by motion alone.</p>

<h2>Motion in the app today</h2>
<p>The renderer rides one three-token scale (160, 360, 700&thinsp;ms) and two easings at about thirty sites, all through tokens, none literal. Hovers, carets and the composer's lift take 160; the dock widths, the switch, the card's edges and the picture viewer take 360; the Firstmate's dim-and-blur behind cards, the quota bars and the whisper's dissolve take 700. Everything that <em>appears</em> is instant.</p>
