<div class="dir" data-default>
<p><b>A. Reveal only</b> (the one I would take). Only what appears gets motion, and only as a fade with a drift of at most 4&thinsp;px on the tokens the app already has. No size tweens, no exits, no JS measuring: two shared keyframes and one reduced-motion rule. Everything animated is opacity or transform, so it costs the compositor nothing over the acrylic card.</p>
</div>
<div class="dir">
<p><b>B. Reveal and continuity.</b> A on top of motion that connects two states: the settings tab underline slides, paging a card slides it 8&thinsp;px in the arrow's direction, a card's height and a dock's collapse tween. Each needs measurement in JS or layout animation, each fights an existing rule (the card's edge rule, the stillness helpers in the end-to-end suite, the resize feedback loop fixed this week), and each can be added later on top of A.</p>
</div>
<div class="dir">
<p><b>C. Adopt transitions.dev.</b> Import its token file and its recipes (accordion by grid rows, sliding tabs, text swap with blur, thinking-state shimmer, toasts). It brings a second scale beside the skill's, blur and scale on card contents over the one blur layer the window allows, and bounces the skill forbids; the app has no toasts or spinners to receive most of it. Not recommended, and nothing in A depends on it.</p>
</div>

<h2>The spec for A</h2>
