    root.style.setProperty("--pv-drift", s.driftPx + "px");
    if (s.reducedMotion) root.setAttribute("data-reduced", ""); else root.removeAttribute("data-reduced");
    var json = JSON.stringify(s);
    hidden.value = json;
    if (window.fm && typeof window.fm.stage === "function") { try { window.fm.stage("preview", s); } catch (e) {} }
  }
  function replay() {
    var card = slot.firstElementChild;
    var copy = card.cloneNode(true);
    slot.removeChild(card);
    void slot.offsetWidth;
    slot.appendChild(copy);
    body.hidden = true;
    disc.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
    window.setTimeout(function () { disc.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true"); body.hidden = false; }, 420);
    window.setTimeout(function () {
      var row = document.createElement("div");
      row.className = "f";
      row.setAttribute("data-fresh", "");
      row.innerHTML = "<i></i><span></span>";
      row.lastChild.textContent = lines[next++ % lines.length];
      feed.appendChild(row);
      while (feed.querySelectorAll(".f").length > 4) feed.removeChild(feed.querySelector(".f"));
    }, 900);
  }
  disc.addEventListener("click", function () {
    var open = disc.getAttribute("aria-expanded") === "true";
    disc.setAttribute("aria-expanded", open ? "false" : "true");
    body.hidden = open;
  });
  root.querySelector("[data-pv-replay]").addEventListener("click", replay);
  root.addEventListener("change", snapshot);
  snapshot();
})();
</script>

<h2>Questions I can answer now</h2>
<fieldset><legend>1. Under reduced motion, do alpha-only fades survive, or does everything become a cut?</legend>
<label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="fades-survive" checked> Fades survive: a fade of 160&thinsp;ms or less may opt in through a <span class="t">--dur-fade</span> token the rule leaves alone; every drift, size and blur goes to zero. (The whisper's approved reduced-motion behaviour is exactly this.)</label>
<label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="all-instant"> Everything instant, as transitions.dev's guard does.</label>
<div class="mine">My answer: fades survive. WCAG's motion criterion is about movement, not opacity, and the whisper already ships this way with the captain's go.</div>
</fieldset>
<fieldset><legend>2. How long is a surface entrance (a card coming to the front, a reply landing)?</legend>
<label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="360" checked> 360&thinsp;ms (<span class="t">--dur-med</span>): the skill's pace for a surface.</label>
<label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="160"> 160&thinsp;ms (<span class="t">--dur-fast</span>): the same clock as in-place reveals.</label>
<div class="mine">My answer: 360 for surfaces, 160 for everything in place (bodies, sections, feed lines, frames). Two clocks, both already tokens.</div>
</fieldset>
<fieldset><legend>3. When the pager moves, does the card slide in the arrow's direction?</legend>
<label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="rise" checked> No: the same fade-and-rise whichever way; the stack is a pile, not pages side by side, and the arrows already say the direction.</label>
<label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="directional"> Yes: an 8&thinsp;px slide from the arrow's side (direction B).</label>
<div class="mine">My answer: no direction. Example of the difference: pressing "Next card" fades the next card up into place, versus the next card sliding in from the right while the current one is cut.</div>
</fieldset>
<fieldset><legend>4. At launch, does the first paint animate?</legend>
<label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="static" checked> No: only what is born after a document's first paint animates; the panels, the first card and the feed's snapshot are simply there.</label>
<label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="fade-in"> Yes: the panels and the first card fade in once at launch.</label>
<div class="mine">My answer: static. It matches the feed's rule that the boot snapshot is not news, and it keeps launch quiet.</div>
</fieldset>
<fieldset><legend>5. Does the design skill's motion line change to name each token's use?</legend>
<label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="revise" checked> Yes: "160 in place, 360 for a surface, 700 for a dissolve; closes are cuts" replaces "360 to 700&thinsp;ms", since the app already uses 160 at a dozen sites.</label>
<label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="keep"> No: leave the line; add only the reduced-motion rule and the two keyframes.</label>
<div class="mine">My answer: revise. The skill is the one home for motion rules and must be extended before the code is (project rule), and the line is already behind the code.</div>
</fieldset>
<fieldset><legend>6. Does "across fm3000" include the frames: agent-written card bodies and the panels a person adds?</legend>
<label><input type="radio" name="q6" value="frames-too" checked> Yes, through the shared frame stylesheet only: a <span class="t">details</span> in a card body or a personal panel gets the same reveal and the same reduced-motion rule; an agent's own script is never touched.</label>
<label><input type="radio" name="q6" value="host-only"> No: the host's chrome only; frames keep their current stylesheet.</label>
<div class="mine">My answer: yes. Example: opening "evidence" on a crewmate's card would settle in like a Tasks group does; with "no", it stays a cut while the group beside it eases.</div>
</fieldset>
<p>Waiting for the next round: if fades survive (1), whether the whisper's 700&thinsp;ms dissolve keeps its length under reduced motion or shortens to the fade token; if B is chosen, whether the tab underline slides or the section alone moves.</p>

